1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,600
In space, a changing signal can tell more than one story.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,100
The environment may have changed.

3
00:00:08,190 --> 00:00:10,200
The body may be responding.

4
00:00:10,250 --> 00:00:13,200
Or the sensor may have lost reliable contact.

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:19,900
Meet ASTER: an offline-capable research console that brings those possibilities into view.

6
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:26,600
Breathing and Exposure aligns local carbon dioxide measurements with the respiratory cycle.

7
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:32,600
It explores what the crew member may be inhaling, while showing the limits of the estimate.

8
00:00:32,730 --> 00:00:36,900
Sensor Integrity checks the quality of the measurement connection.

9
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:48,300
When contact becomes unreliable, ASTER marks the affected interpretation instead of presenting uncertain data as a confident conclusion.

10
00:00:48,490 --> 00:00:58,900
Exercise and Muscle Response compares similar activity under similar loads, with mechanical signals and fluid-sensitive measurements kept in context.

11
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:05,600
Each event connects observations, quality checks, and versioned rules to an inspectable explanation.

12
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:10,100
When the ground link is unavailable, the case remains accessible locally.

13
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:19,200
When communication resumes, its evidence can be delivered and reviewed, with replies linked to the correct revision.

14
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:28,700
A reproducible simulation lab lets researchers challenge these methods with noise, missing data, and conflicting signals.

15
00:01:28,890 --> 00:01:34,300
ASTER is a research prototype, demonstrated with simulated data.

16
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:40,800
Its purpose is to make the evidence, the uncertainty, and the next research step visible.

